# Marginalisation: From Posterior Landscape to Scientific Answer

Group: Bayesian Statistics & Posterior Inference

Open index.html directly, or serve it alongside TeachingTools. All mathematics
and Canvas rendering run locally with no R, Shiny, WebR or runtime downloads.
The existing app.R and model.R are retained unchanged.

Lecture: https://anguswright.github.io/LectureNotes/IntroductionToStatistics_Section3b_html.html

## Model and interpretation

The normalised posterior is a two-component correlated Gaussian mixture.
The narrow component has mean (-1,0), SDs (0.23,0.3); the broad component has
mean (1.3,1), equal adjustable SDs, and weight w. Both have correlation 0.45.
The exact marginal is (1-w)*Normal(x|-1,0.23) + w*Normal(x|1.3,width).
The selected interval has width 0.6 and its exact probability uses weighted
Normal-CDF differences, integrated over every real nuisance y.

The heatmap contains density, not per-pixel probability. Its finite-window
integral uses midpoint sums with dx*dy; numerical column integration uses dy.
Neither integration nor interval mass uses a maximum/profile. The tallest mode
can belong to the component with the smaller total mass.

## Intentional differences from R

- The analytic model, defaults, guided cases and lecture are unchanged.
- The image is a 180x180 cell-centred grid rather than 250x250 endpoints.
  Density contours are omitted; square-root-stretched colours are retained.
- A labelled finite-window probability is added, without renormalising the
  omitted tails outside x in [-5,7], y in [-6,8]. Numerical window mass and
  grid mode are approximations; the marginal and interval remain analytic.
- An optional grid profile is normalised over the displayed x window only
  to compare shapes. It is explicitly not a posterior marginal or an alternate
  interval probability.
- The seed and Re-evaluate action are intentionally inert mathematically:
  this lesson is deterministic, so no artificial simulation or animation is
  introduced. The common seed validation/range remains available.

## Files and exports

Runtime: index.html, model.js, app.js, and shared TeachingTools/statistics.js,
browser.js, lesson.js, browser.css. No additional runtime assets.
Pure CommonJS/browser global MarginalisationModel exports mixtureJoint,
mixtureMarginal, mixtureInterval, integrateGrid and compute.
compute parameters: seed, weight, width, centre, show_profile.
integrateGrid returns y-major joint rows, x/y cell centres, dx/dy, numerical
column integrals and window mass. Invalid inputs throw Astro.ValidationError.

## Validation

From the repository root, with Node 22+ and Chrome:

    node Marginalisation/test_model.js
    mkdir -p Marginalisation/.browser-profile
    env -u HTML_SITE_ROOT TMPDIR="$PWD/Marginalisation/.browser-profile" node Marginalisation/test_browser.js
    rmdir Marginalisation/.browser-profile

Numerical tests compare retained R density/CDF values, independently integrate
joint columns and the marginal, test the narrow-component 68.268949% interval,
and compare two grid resolutions against an independent R conditional-Normal
window integral (w=.7, width=2: 0.99759719104748223).
They also distinguish profiling from integration and cover invalid settings.

    Rscript -e 'source("Marginalisation/model.R"); print(mixture_interval(.9,1.5,.7,1.5),digits=17)'

This returns 0.11072054431171223. Browser tests inspect real plot colours,
profile visibility, fixed joint density when only the selected interval changes,
wide-component omitted mass and seed invariance, plus the shared presets,
reset, invalid seed, keyboard tabs, mobile and offline-file checks.
